__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/

# Local credentials. Never committed. Create it yourself:
#   printf 'GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...\n' > .env.local
# llm.py reads this file directly, so nothing has to be exported first.
.env.local
.env

# The rendered slides. Instagram fetches each image once, at container creation,
# and serves the post from its own CDN afterwards, so the copy in git is written
# and never read. Nine PNGs is 5.6 MB per deck, twice a day, in permanent
# history. What is worth keeping is the markdown, the published id and the
# contact sheet — and by invariant 8 the contact sheet is what a deck is judged
# by anyway.
#
# One exception, and auto-post.yml makes it: a deck held for review can be
# posted days later from a fresh checkout, and post_to_ig.py builds its URLs
# from the local filenames, so the slides of anything still in state/pending are
# force-added.
carousels/*/slides/*.png

# The per-deck mascot working folder, for the same reason and a bigger number.
#
# With generation on by default (invariant 2), every deck writes the raw frame
# and the library candidate for each of its nine poses into carousels/<slug>/
# mascot/. That measured 2.6 MB for two generated poses, so about 7.3 MB a deck
# and ~15 MB a day in permanent history — the same order as the slides problem
# that invariant 18 exists to stop, arriving through a different folder.
#
# Nothing is lost by ignoring it. A generated pose ALSO goes into
# mascot/library/ through import_poses.py, where the library's own gates and
# matte decide, and that copy is committed and reused by every later deck. The
# per-deck folder is the intermediate, not the artwork.
carousels/*/mascot/

# The gh-pages working copy the retention step clones into. It is the published
# branch, checked out beside the repo, and it is never part of main.
/gh-pages/
